//! member: true
//!
//! std.ops — the operator traits: the protocols `+ - * / %` and unary
//! `-` dispatch through when their operand is a type parameter or a user
//! type. Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **This module is what `[type.trait.op]` asks std for, and the shapes
//! are the clause's, not this module's opinion.** Since s155 (wolf-lang
//! #5, spec/10 §11) an operator whose left operand is a type parameter
//! or a user type is a call to a trait method chosen BY NAME: `a + b` is
//! `Add.add(a, b)`, `-a` is `Neg.neg(a)`. The compiler refuses any other
//! method shape with E0514, a missing impl with E0502 and no trait in
//! scope with E0301, so the six declarations below are transcriptions
//! and a rename here is a break for every caller.
//!
//! **On primitives the impls ARE the builtin operations** — the clause
//! says so in as many words, so `[T: Add]` instantiated at `int` is the
//! machine add and nothing is interposed. Every body below is one
//! operator expression for that reason; a body that did arithmetic of
//! its own would be a different function wearing the operator's name.
//!
//! **`Eq` and `Ord` are NOT here.** They are `std.cmp`'s and they stay
//! there: `Ordering` is one enum in std (API-CONVENTIONS §6) and the
//! compiler reads `Ord.cmp`'s answer by VARIANT NAME, so a second
//! `Ordering` would be a second answer to a settled question. A bound
//! that wants both writes `[T: ops.Add + cmp.Ord]`; the composed alias
//! `Num` is the next paragraph's subject.
//!
//! **`trait Num` IS NOT SHIPPED AT THESE PINS, and it is the one item
//! of s155's surface this module withholds.** `[type.trait.op.alias]`
//! spells it `trait Num = Add + Sub + Mul + Div + Rem + Eq + Ord`, and
//! wolf-std#26 asks for it by name. Two measurements at sc44 say not
//! yet, and the second is the one that decides:
//!
//! 1. The alias FORM is `E0201: expected `{`, found `=`
//!    [gram.item.trait]` under lupin 0.1.33, whose conformance pin is
//!    v0.2.10 — one release short of s155. A parse failure is not a
//!    row's verdict, it is the FILE, so an alias committed anywhere in
//!    this module's directory would take the reference machine's lane
//!    off every importer of the six traits above.
//! 2. Parking it in the nursery does not pay that bill either, and
//!    this is the part that is not obvious: `tests/ledger.toml`'s
//!    lupin column is `run | unsupported | slow | divergent(…)` and
//!    REFUSES `fail(…)` by name, so a resident whose module file
//!    fails to PARSE on lupin has no legal row to be ledgered with.
//!    A body with no ledgerable test is a claim rather than code
//!    (D31's register, rule 1 — `std.x.json.float_at` was written and
//!    withdrawn inside one sprint for exactly that), so `Num` is
//!    withdrawn here rather than shipped anywhere.
//!
//! **The trigger, named:** the wolf-interp release whose conformance
//! pin is at or past wolf-lang `c9237c1` (v0.2.11). On that release the
//! alias goes in above, verbatim from the clause, and this paragraph
//! becomes a CHANGELOG line. `Num` at `f64` needs a second thing as
//! well — see `Rem` below — so the two triggers are independent and
//! `Num` at `int` is the one that lands first.
//!
//! INTERIM SPELLINGS (each filed, flipped by the pin bump that fixes it):
//! - **`impl Rem for f64` is NOT shipped.** Its body is `self % other`
//!   by the clause, and `%` on `f64` has no native lowering
//!   (`unsupported — this float operator (no frem op)`) and no checked
//!   one either (`unsupported — `%` on floats (unruled: fmod vs
//!   remainder)`). Measured at sc44, and the measurement is the reason
//!   rather than the story: with the impl present and NOTHING CALLING
//!   IT, a program that imports this module and adds two ints goes from
//!   `exit(0)` to `unsupported` on the native lane. One refused body
//!   costs every importer a lane (house doctrine, sc01), so it waits for
//!   wolf-lang#327. The consequence is stated where it lands:
//!   `std.x.ops_num`'s `Num` is satisfied at `int` and not at `f64`.
//! - `impl Div for int` is integer division, truncating toward zero, and
//!   it traps `divide_by_zero` on a zero divisor exactly as the operator
//!   does — the impl IS the operator, traps included.
//! - Every example in this module is PROSE and not a fenced block, for a
//!   reason measured at sc44 and filed at wolf-interp: a qualified trait
//!   in a BOUND (`fn f[T: ops.Add]`) does not count as a use of `use
//!   std.ops` under lupin 0.1.33, so the import the extractor writes is
//!   `E0305 … never used [mod.use.unused]` — a hard error on a name that
//!   IS used, and the compiler accepts the same file. §4's doc-truth
//!   rule wants the reference machine at `exit(0)`; until that is fixed
//!   no block here can reach it, and an example that cannot run belongs
//!   in prose.

/// Addition, `a + b`. The compiler dispatches `+` here when the left
/// operand is a type parameter bounded by `Add` or a user type with an
/// `Add` impl in scope ([type.trait.op]); both operands are the same
/// type, which the clause calls homogeneity and E0514 enforces.
///
/// Prose, not a fenced example (see the module header, wolf-interp's
/// bound-is-not-a-use): under `fn total[T: ops.Add](xs: List[T], zero:
/// T)`, `acc = acc + x` is `Add.add(acc, x)`, and at `int` that is the
/// machine add.
pub trait Add {
    fn add(self, other: Self) -> Self
}

/// Subtraction, `a - b`. Binary only: unary minus is `Neg`.
pub trait Sub {
    fn sub(self, other: Self) -> Self
}

/// Multiplication, `a * b`.
pub trait Mul {
    fn mul(self, other: Self) -> Self
}

/// Division, `a / b`. The impl inherits the operator's traps: at `int`
/// a zero divisor traps `divide_by_zero`, at `f64` it is IEEE and traps
/// nothing.
pub trait Div {
    fn div(self, other: Self) -> Self
}

/// Remainder, `a % b`. At `int` the sign follows the dividend
/// (truncated remainder) and a zero divisor traps `divide_by_zero`.
/// There is no `f64` impl at these pins — see the module header.
pub trait Rem {
    fn rem(self, other: Self) -> Self
}

/// Arithmetic negation, unary `-a`. One argument, unlike the five
/// binary traits, and the clause reads the shape: a `neg` taking two
/// parameters is E0514.
pub trait Neg {
    fn neg(self) -> Self
}

impl Add for int {
    fn add(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self + other
    }
}

impl Sub for int {
    fn sub(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self - other
    }
}

impl Mul for int {
    fn mul(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self * other
    }
}

impl Div for int {
    fn div(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self / other
    }
}

impl Rem for int {
    fn rem(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self % other
    }
}

impl Neg for int {
    fn neg(self) -> Self {
        -self
    }
}

impl Add for f64 {
    fn add(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self + other
    }
}

impl Sub for f64 {
    fn sub(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self - other
    }
}

impl Mul for f64 {
    fn mul(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self * other
    }
}

impl Div for f64 {
    fn div(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self / other
    }
}

impl Neg for f64 {
    fn neg(self) -> Self {
        -self
    }
}

// NOT SHIPPED at these pins — `impl Rem for f64`, whose body is
// `self % other` by [type.trait.op]. Both compiler tiers refuse that
// expression (`no frem op` natively, `unruled: fmod vs remainder`
// checked) and the refusal is not local: with the impl present and
// nothing calling it, an importer that adds two ints drops from
// `exit(0)` to `unsupported` on the native lane. wolf-lang#327 is the
// trigger; the maintainer's call there is between lowering `%` on
// floats and ruling it out, and either answer lands this impl or
// retires the line.

/// Concatenation, `a + b` ([type.str.concat]). `str`'s one operator
/// impl: there is no `Sub`, `Mul`, `Div` or `Rem` for `str`, because a
/// string has no such operation and an impl that invented one would be
/// a lie the operator table tells for it.
impl Add for str {
    fn add(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self + other
    }
}

// BLOCKED — the bitwise family (`BitAnd`, `BitOr`, `BitXor`, `Shl`,
// `Shr`, `Not`) and `Index`. [type.trait.op]'s table this edition is the
// six arithmetic operators plus `Eq`/`Ord`'s comparisons; nothing
// upstream dispatches `&`, `|`, `^`, `<<`, `>>` or `xs[i]` through a
// trait, so a trait named for one would be a declaration no compiler
// reads. They land with the clause that adds them to the table.
